  Also two extra findings:
  1. STAMP basename dependence: `SELF = basename(__file__)` — a renamed copy of the counter always gets STAMP committed=none when its embedded counterpart isn't present under that name. Tamper evidence works via name+hash.
  2. STAMP is self-consistent inside an attacker-controlled fixture root: embedding the mutated counter into the fixture makes STAMP green — i.e., STAMP binds the run to "the counter committed in the tree being evaluated", not to an external authority. In the real lane run, the committed counter is the unmutated one, so the four-state acceptance in [C] cannot happen against the lane: `python3 accept-d.py --root .` with mutated script → STAMP self≠committed → FAIL. So even the mutated-counter attack against the real lane tree is caught by STAMP. Let me actually verify that quickly — run MUT2 against the LANE root. Wait: careful — running the counter against the lane root is read-only, fine. MUT2 vs lane root: SECTION will fail? The lane SKILL.md doesn't have the table yet (not landed), so SECTION FAIL and everything cascades. STAMP will FAIL (mutated vs committed hash). That demonstrates: mutating the counter doesn't help an attacker pass against the real lane, both because STAMP fails and because the table isn't there. Let me run it for completeness — read-only on the lane.

